   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to authorizing\npowers of the housing trust fund corporation, and specifying the scope of\neligible purchasers of the housing finance agency and the New York city\nhousing development corporation bonds",
    "basePrintNo": "S7149",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7149-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1102, 44 & 654, Priv Hous Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7149\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to author-\nizing powers of the housing trust fund corporation, and specifying the\nscope of eligible purchasers of the housing finance agency and the New\nYork city housing development corporation bonds\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would amend the Housing Finance Agency (HFA) and Housing\nDevelopment Corporation (HDC) statutes to broaden the scope of eligible\npurchasers of non-rated, privately issued HFA and HDC bonds to include\nagencies that are active in the housing space. Lastly, this bill would\nalso amend the statute of HFA's subsidiary, the Housing Trust Fund\nCorporation (HTFC), to extend the 30- year loan term maximum by allowing\nthe loan term to be extended to forty years to provide for the timing to\nbe co-terminus with other financing or regulating agencies participating\nin the project with longer financing and regulatory requirements.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Section 1102 of the Private Housing Finance\nLaw to provide that the length of any loan provided by HTFC shall not\nexceed forty years.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends paragraph 3 of subdivision 29-a of Section\n44 of the Private Housing Finance Law to specify that the Federal Home\nLoan Mortgage Corporation, the Federal National Mortgage Association,\nthe Governmental National Mortgage Association and any successor thereto\nare included within the definition of \"governmental agency of the United\nStates\" found in paragraph 3 of subdivision 29-a.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends paragraph 3 of subdivision 23-c of Section\n654 of the Private Housing Finance Law to specify that the Federal Home\nLoan Mortgage Corporation, the Federal National Mortgage Association,\nthe Governmental National Mortgage Association and any successor thereto\nare included within the definition of \"governmental agency of the United\nStates\" found in paragraph 3 of subdivision 23-c.\n\nSection 4 of the bill provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nHTFC Loan Terms\nUnder current law, HTFC is authorized to enter into contracts with\neligible applicants for the furnishing by such applicants of housing for\npersons of low income, provided that each such contract, among other\nthings, provided that the length of any loan (the \"Tenn Loan\nRestriction\") shall not exceed thirty years.\n\nHFA and HDC statutory amendments\nCurrent law does not permit HFA or HDC to issue unrated bonds for direct\npurchase to Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corporation (Freddie Mac), the\nFederal National Mortgage Association (Fannie Mae), the Governmental\nNational Mortgage Association (Ginnie Mae), since they are not consid-\nered governmental agencies of the United States.\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHTFC Loan Terms\n\nAs stated previously, HTFC is authorized to enter into contracts for the\nfurnishing by such applicants of housing for persons of low income,\nprovided that each such contract, among other things, provided that the\nTerm Loan Restriction not exceed thirty years.  There are several\nfactors that underlie the request to extend the term of HTFC's subordi-\nnate loan programs, including the following:\n\na) Matching the term of the subordinate loan to the first mortgage.\nFirst mortgage products can be longer than 30 years, most often 35.  In\nsome cases projects may not be able to benefit from such programs as a\nresult of the Term Loan Restriction Provision. Longer first mortgage\nterms are sometimes beneficial to the project and help reduce state\nresources needed for the project\n\n(b) Assuring value exceeds the amount of subordinate debt. In distressed\nmarkets the projected value of the property in 30 years may not exceed\nthe value of the subordinate financing, which causes technical problems\nwith the financing of projects. The ability to extend the mortgage term\nis one strategy to deal with this issue.\n\n(c) Extending regulatory periods. The ability to have longer subordinate\nfinancing term supports the state's goal of creating and preserving\naffordable housing as HCR can require longer regulatory periods to\naccompany the longer financing tenn.\n\n(d) To assure the supportive housing requirement for a continuation of\nuse for 40 years. The limitation of 30 years on the subordinate debt\nmakes this requirement problematic.\n\n(e) to ensure that loans could be co-terminus with more regulatory\nagreements pursuant to the Low Income Housing Tax Credit, which may last\nfor fifty years.\n\nHFA and HDC statutory amendments\n\nThe bill would also amend the HFA and HDC statutes to allow them to\nutilize additional financing structures offered by Freddie Mac, Fannie\nMae, Ginnie Mae, and any successors thereto.\n\nIn 2011, the HFA and HDC statutes were amended to authorize the issuance\nof unrated bonds for direct purchase by certain financial institutions,\nincluding governmental agencies of the United States.  As these finan-\ncial institutions would assume the real estate credit risk associated\nwith these transactions, the need for HFA and HDC to obtain costly cred-\nit enhancements was eliminated. Since 2011, both HFA and HDC have taken\nadvantage of these changes and lowered the cost of bonds issued by both\nagencies.\n\nHFA and HDC now seek to amend the 2011 legislation to clarify and speci-\nfy that certain government agencies (the Federal Home Loan Mortgage\nCorporation, the Federal National Mortgage Association, the Governmental\nNational Mortgage Association, and any successors thereto) all are\nincluded within the term \"governmental agency of the United States\",\nwhich is one of the categories of permissible \"financial institutions\"\neligible under the law to purchase privately unrated HFA and HDC bonds\nand loans. This change is needed in order to ensure that these entities,\nall of whom are federal governmental agencies, but with differing offi-\ncial US government status, are covered by the term \"governmental agency\nof the United States\" under the HFA and HDC statutes. These governmental\nagencies of the United States are currently very active in the housing\nspace, and their ability to directly purchase unrated HFA and HDC loans\nwould enhance the private placement programs of both agencies and\nfurther lower financing costs for the agencies' respective housing\nprograms.\n\nIn addition, the market dislocations caused by the COVID-19 crisis,\naffect HFA and HDC bonds to trade at higher prices in the capital\nmarkets, thus providing an incentive to developers to whom loans have\nbeen made from those bond proceeds to seek to prepay the HFA and HDC\nbonds and find alternative financing. This has the potential to cause\nlarge losses in fee income to both HFA and HDC, which funds serve as\ncrucial sources of subsidy for affordable financings. If IPA and HDC had\navailable to them the more efficient financing vehicles that having\nFreddie Mac and GNMAE bring, developer clients could still make use of\nour programs at cost efficient terms and would remain within the HFA and\nHDC portfolios.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis bill comprised LBD  09319-04-0 in 2020 (HCR  3-2020); and, prior to\n2020, HCR submitted a bill containing a concept similar to that\ncontained in section 4 of this bill, which passed the Senate as A.  8247\n(Cymbrowitz)/S. 6199 (Jackson) in 2019.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediate.\n",
